WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.327
Here's a strange outage.

2
00:00:04.327 --> 00:00:08.665
A program slows down for five seconds, then gets its speed back.

3
00:00:08.867 --> 00:00:12.751
Yet a minute later, it's still giving useful answers to nobody.

4
00:00:12.751 --> 00:00:15.835
The cause is long gone, so what's keeping it down?

5
00:00:16.033 --> 00:00:20.200
The culprit is the most innocent habit in programming: trying again.

6
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:22.213
Picture a coffee counter.

7
00:00:22.213 --> 00:00:27.903
Each order is a slip on a rail, and the barista makes drinks in the order the slips sit there.

8
00:00:28.067 --> 00:00:32.361
On a normal morning she keeps up, and the rail stays almost empty.

9
00:00:32.567 --> 00:00:33.452
One more rule.

10
00:00:33.452 --> 00:00:39.077
A customer who has waited three minutes gives up, goes back to the till and orders again.

11
00:00:39.077 --> 00:00:41.163
The first slip stays on the rail.

12
00:00:41.367 --> 00:00:44.898
Now the coffee machine runs slowly for a few minutes.

13
00:00:44.898 --> 00:00:45.831
Slips pile up.

14
00:00:45.831 --> 00:00:49.963
People give up and order again, so the pile grows even faster.

15
00:00:50.167 --> 00:00:53.703
Soon the barista is making drinks for people who have left.

16
00:00:53.703 --> 00:00:56.939
Then the machine recovers, and it makes no difference.

17
00:00:56.939 --> 00:00:59.936
The reorders keep coming faster than she can work.

18
00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:03.492
She's busier than ever, serving a café full of ghosts.

19
00:01:03.492 --> 00:01:07.324
So the slowdown is over, but the reorders keep the rail full.

20
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:08.879
Programs do the same.

21
00:01:08.879 --> 00:01:12.426
The barista is the server, the program doing the work.

22
00:01:12.426 --> 00:01:13.872
The rail is its queue.

23
00:01:13.872 --> 00:01:17.681
The customers are clients, programs that send it requests.

24
00:01:17.867 --> 00:01:22.495
Three minutes is the timeout, how long a client waits before giving up.

25
00:01:22.495 --> 00:01:24.451
And ordering again is a retry.

26
00:01:25.167 --> 00:01:26.444
Now with numbers.

27
00:01:26.444 --> 00:01:30.953
The server can finish 1,000 requests a second, and gets 800.

28
00:01:30.953 --> 00:01:34.936
So it's 80% busy, and answers in about a millisecond.

29
00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:42.061
Clients give up on an attempt after 200 milliseconds and try again at once, up to 4 attempts in all.

30
00:01:42.267 --> 00:01:46.624
At ten seconds in, something the server depends on slows down.

31
00:01:46.624 --> 00:01:50.138
For five seconds, it can finish only 400 a second.

32
00:01:50.138 --> 00:01:51.825
Then it's back to 1,000.

33
00:01:51.825 --> 00:01:53.933
When does the service recover?

34
00:01:53.933 --> 00:01:55.057
Pause and guess.

35
00:01:58.233 --> 00:01:59.060
Let's watch.

36
00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:04.436
In a fifth of a second, 80 are queued: 200 milliseconds of work at this speed.

37
00:02:04.436 --> 00:02:08.088
So anything joining now times out before it's served.

38
00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:13.322
Every attempt times out, so every request is sent four times.

39
00:02:13.322 --> 00:02:17.273
800 new requests become 3,200 attempts a second.

40
00:02:17.467 --> 00:02:19.771
The server can't tell who's still waiting.

41
00:02:19.771 --> 00:02:24.819
It works through the queue in order, and every answer goes to a client that already gave up.

42
00:02:25.033 --> 00:02:28.730
At fifteen seconds, the server is back to full speed.

43
00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:30.335
It makes no difference.

44
00:02:30.335 --> 00:02:35.357
3,200 attempts arrive each second, more than three times what it can do.

45
00:02:35.533 --> 00:02:42.568
A minute in, 111,557 attempts are queued, and not one answer is useful.

46
00:02:42.568 --> 00:02:43.261
Well...

47
00:02:43.261 --> 00:02:46.135
that was a long five seconds.

48
00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:50.613
The rate of answers someone is still waiting for is called goodput.

49
00:02:50.613 --> 00:02:52.700
Here it's zero, and it stays zero.

50
00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:59.493
A trigger pushed the system into a bad state, and its own behaviour keeps it there after the trigger is gone.

51
00:02:59.493 --> 00:03:01.610
That's called a metastable failure.

52
00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:05.784
Getting out takes a strong push, like cutting the load or restarting.

53
00:03:05.933 --> 00:03:07.130
So what fixes it?

54
00:03:07.130 --> 00:03:16.137
The obvious idea: wait longer before each retry, doubling the wait each time, with a random part so clients don't retry in step.

55
00:03:16.137 --> 00:03:18.881
That's exponential backoff with jitter.

56
00:03:19.067 --> 00:03:21.636
To be fair, it spreads the retries out.

57
00:03:21.636 --> 00:03:25.655
But every attempt still times out, so there are just as many.

58
00:03:25.655 --> 00:03:27.236
This run never recovers.

59
00:03:27.433 --> 00:03:28.697
No retries at all?

60
00:03:28.697 --> 00:03:30.523
It recovers at 24 seconds.

61
00:03:30.523 --> 00:03:35.859
But then every everyday hiccup, like a dropped connection, becomes an error.

62
00:03:36.033 --> 00:03:42.132
A better rule is a retry budget: retries may add at most 10% to the work coming in.

63
00:03:42.132 --> 00:03:45.732
Now the server never gets more than 880 a second.

64
00:03:45.933 --> 00:03:47.699
It recovers at 34 seconds.

65
00:03:47.699 --> 00:03:52.859
Slowly, because it still serves a backlog of attempts nobody is waiting for.

66
00:03:53.067 --> 00:03:54.236
So drop those too.

67
00:03:54.236 --> 00:03:58.457
Picture a barista who throws away slips older than three minutes.

68
00:03:58.457 --> 00:04:06.249
In a program, each request carries its deadline, and the server drops attempts that are past it without working on them.

69
00:04:06.433 --> 00:04:08.506
Clients still retry four times.

70
00:04:08.506 --> 00:04:12.584
During the slowdown, all 400 answers a second are now useful.

71
00:04:12.584 --> 00:04:18.400
And the moment the server is back to full speed, at fifteen seconds, so is the service.

72
00:04:18.967 --> 00:04:20.902
So retries aren't the villain.

73
00:04:20.902 --> 00:04:23.353
The trouble is retries under overload.

74
00:04:23.567 --> 00:04:28.801
And here the café picture breaks: a real customer reorders once and goes home.

75
00:04:28.801 --> 00:04:32.156
A program retries four times and never gets tired.

76
00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:34.799
So why was it still down a minute later?

77
00:04:34.799 --> 00:04:39.609
Its own clients kept it down, asking again for answers nobody would wait for.

78
00:04:39.833 --> 00:04:43.488
Give retries a budget, and drop work nobody waits for.

79
00:04:44.067 --> 00:04:45.251
One quick check.

80
00:04:45.251 --> 00:04:51.841
With a 10% budget and 800 new requests a second, what's the most the server is ever sent?

81
00:04:51.841 --> 00:04:53.025
Pause and think.

82
00:04:56.233 --> 00:05:01.061
880 a second: the 800 new ones, plus 10% more.
